# Intent: The asset store, device side (owner decision 17; core SF89; design docs/plan/installed-set.md B and G). The part of the assets partition from CONFIG_MAIPAI_ASSET_STORE_START (0x320000) to the pack cache holds installed assets of every kind; the host installs with the tools self.assets.list, install, data, commit, remove, swap and abort (every refusal says what went wrong and what to do, in English and Spanish, with a code). Signed kinds need the release key's Ed25519 signature over the 128 byte header (the owner's own kinds, like look, are taken from the paired host on the hash and length); the body is streamed in 2,048 byte pieces, written a sector at a time with a pause between sectors, checked, read back and hashed, and only then does the directory flip. A game, an update, a lock or another install refuses it; so does a largest internal block under 20,000 bytes. Events asset_progress (every 10 percent), asset_done and asset_failed. A consumer calls maipai_assets_slots and maipai_assets_load (a PSRAM copy it frees). Off unless MAIPAI_ASSET_STORE (the nofont and lean images turn it on; the plain image is too large for it); with it on the build refuses an assets image that reaches the store.
# Placement: unit
# Upstream: firmware/main/hal/maipai_assets.cc and maipai_assets.h (ours); a registration line in hal_mcp.cpp; a build limit in main/CMakeLists.txt
# Check after a flash: on a nofont or lean image, self.assets.list shows empty kinds and the free room (336 sectors less 8 for the directory); installing a signed game pack with install, data, commit shows it in the list as ok and active; a flipped byte in the body is refused at commit with the hash error and the list is unchanged; remove frees the room; a cut of the power in the middle of an install leaves the old list after the reboot; self.assets.install during a game is refused in words.
# Kconfig: MAIPAI_ASSET_STORE (default n), MAIPAI_ASSET_STORE_START (hex, default 0x320000)
# Memory cost: nothing while off or unused. In use: one 12.5 KB block in PSRAM (the directory, a 4 KB sector being filled, a 4 KB scratch and a piece), freed after a minute of no use; internal RAM about 100 bytes (a mutex, a timer, flags); flash about 7 KB (estimate).

